{"hash":"a33528aa3941bab6","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":87.0,"said":"Jai Shri Mahakal! Come, walk with me. The line moves slowly here, so let me tell you where you are. Behind us lies Shri Mahakal Lok, the long corridor of carved pillars and painted stories from the Shiva Purana, opened in October 2022. But this city was holy long before any corridor. This is Ujjain, on the river Kshipra, the city the ancients called Avantika, one of India’s seven sacred cities. Some fifteen hundred years ago, the poet Kalidasa came this way in his imagination. In his poem the Meghaduta, a lonely lover sends a rain-cloud across India with a message, and as it passes over Ujjayini he asks it to wait at Mahakala until sunset, so that its thunder can be the drum at Shiva’s evening worship. Listen to the bells ahead; that worship still goes on. And notice how the people here speak of him. To Ujjain, Mahakal is not only God. He is their king, the true ruler of this city. Why a king? The answer lies below. Our path is about to go down into the earth, and so does the oldest story of Mahakal.\n\nDid you know? Every twelve years, when Jupiter enters Leo, Ujjain hosts the Simhastha Kumbh on the Kshipra; the next is due in 2028.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1015},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1031,"end":1149}],"words":[[0.098,0.3,0,3],[0.411,0.252,4,8],[0.675,0.661,9,16],[2.26,0.433,18,22],[2.861,0.312,24,28],[3.185,0.204,29,33],[3.401,0.288,34,36],[4.555,0.108,38,41],[4.676,0.276,42,46],[4.964,0.3,47,52],[5.276,0.469,53,59],[5.757,0.325,60,64],[6.346,0.192,66,68],[6.551,0.24,69,72],[6.803,0.108,73,75],[6.923,0.24,76,80],[7.176,0.12,81,84],[7.308,0.216,85,90],[7.536,0.12,91,94],[7.668,0.288,95,98],[8.894,0.421,100,106],[9.327,0.204,107,109],[9.543,0.252,110,114],[9.808,0.252,115,119],[10.072,0.493,120,127],[10.577,0.361,128,131],[11.226,0.084,133,136],[11.322,0.337,137,141],[11.671,0.505,142,150],[12.188,0.156,151,153],[12.356,0.349,154,160],[12.716,0.4